::ENG1_U02_L01_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG1.R.01] Passage: The hallway clock ticked loudly as Mara waited outside the principal's office. She twisted the edge of her permission slip until it wrinkled. Question: Which literary element is most important in building tension?{
=A. Setting#Correct. The hallway and ticking clock create a tense place where Mara waits.
~B. Theme#Incorrect. The question asks for the element building tension, not the message of the passage.
~C. Rhyme#Incorrect. The passage is prose and does not use rhyme.
~D. Dialogue#Incorrect. No character speaks in the passage.
}

::ENG1_U02_L01_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG1.R.01] Passage: The garden behind the apartment building had one bright sunflower growing through a crack in the concrete. Question: What layer of meaning does the setting add?{
~A. The building is newly painted.#Incorrect. The passage does not mention paint.
~B. The character dislikes flowers.#Incorrect. No character reaction is given.
=C. Hope can appear in a difficult place.#Correct. The sunflower growing through concrete suggests life and hope in a hard setting.
~D. The garden has many trees.#Incorrect. The passage only mentions one sunflower.
}

::ENG1_U02_L01_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG1.R.01] Passage: Theo wanted to apologize, but every time he saw his brother, he remembered the broken model airplane on the floor. Question: Which conflict is shown?{
~A. Theo is lost in a strange city.#Incorrect. The passage does not show a travel problem.
=B. Theo wants to repair a relationship but struggles with guilt or memory.#Correct. His desire to apologize conflicts with the painful reminder.
~C. Theo wants to win a race.#Incorrect. There is no race in the passage.
~D. Theo is angry about homework.#Incorrect. Homework is not mentioned.
}

::ENG1_U02_L01_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG1.R.01] Passage: I kept the ticket stub in my pocket all summer because it reminded me of the day Dad came home. Question: Which point of view is used?{
~A. Third-person limited#Incorrect. The narrator uses I, so the story is not third person.
~B. Second person#Incorrect. The narrator does not speak directly to you.
~C. Third-person omniscient#Incorrect. The passage does not use he, she, or they as the narrator position.
=D. First person#Correct. The narrator uses I and my to tell the experience.
}

::ENG1_U02_L01_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG1.R.01] Passage: Ava gave away her last pencil, then stared at her own blank page. Question: What does Ava's action reveal?{
~A. Ava is careless because she dislikes school.#Incorrect. The passage does not show that she dislikes school.
=B. Ava is generous, even when helping someone creates a problem for herself.#Correct. She gives away her last pencil and then has none for her own work.
~C. Ava wants to leave class early.#Incorrect. Leaving class is not mentioned.
~D. Ava cannot read the assignment.#Incorrect. The issue is the missing pencil, not reading.
}
